  "caveats": [
    "Current rates are 2025 final adopted rates \u2014 the newest complete set the state publishes. 2026 rates are not certified until after the August hearings.",
    "Eagle Mountain is the one city here whose 2026 rate is settled: the council adopted 0.001500 on August 6, after noticing 0.001700 and hearing nearly three hours of public comment. The charts carry the adopted rate for Eagle Mountain and each other city's noticed figure for the rest, whose hearings run through late August.",
    "Eagle Mountain's tentative schedule (read May 26) implied a rate of 0.001842 and a 245% increase. The rate it noticed was 0.001700, a 220.9% increase measured against the newer certified rate. The rate it adopted is 0.001500, a 183.02% increase on the same basis.",
    "Percent increases are each city's own noticed figure as it was reported when the increase was proposed: the rise in that city's property tax revenue. They are not increases in your total tax bill, and they are not the ratio of proposed collections to current collections — for Eagle Mountain the adopted rate's ratio is 204.5%, against the 183.02% in the motion.",
    "Utah County's combined notice, linked below, is the primary document for this year's proposals, and several rows here differ from it. Highland's and Orem's figures here are the levy being raised rather than the entity-wide increase the notice states (6.89% and 6.95%); Pleasant Grove is furthest apart, 16.55% here against 8.72% on the notice; American Fork, Cedar Hills, Saratoga Springs and Springville each sit within four points of their noticed figure. Lehi and Santaquin proposed increases but do not appear on the proof the city published, and Draper appears on it at 22.55% but is not charted here, being mostly a Salt Lake County city. Eagle Mountain's line matches this page to the dollar: $142.25 to $456.28 on a $488,000 home, $6,742,938, 220.88%.",
    "Highland's and Orem's proposals raise a dedicated levy (library; public safety), not the general city rate.",
    "Draper and Bluffdale levy in Utah County but lie mostly in Salt Lake County.",
    "Resulting rates are each city's current rate plus the rate increase implied by its own published figures (the dollar rise on its stated average home, over 55% of that home's value). This avoids assuming the noticed percentage applies to the general city rate \u2014 for Highland and Orem it does not. Tested against Eagle Mountain, the only city publishing a proposed rate, the method reproduces the 0.001700 it noticed — the 0.001500 in the charts is the rate it went on to adopt, read off the motion rather than derived. Rows marked \u2021 rest on a rounded dollar figure and are approximate."
  ],
