    "caveats": [
      "Rates are each city's 2025 final adopted general levy \u2014 the same basis as the Utah County chart. Eagle Mountain (Utah County) is shown as a dashed reference bar so you can see where its rate falls among Salt Lake County's cities.",
      "Salt Lake County comparisons are not apples-to-apples with Eagle Mountain. Many of its cities fund fire through the Unified Fire Service Area and police through the Salt Lake Valley Law Enforcement Service Area \u2014 countywide levies billed separately from, and on top of, the city rate shown here.",
      "The eight cities and towns marked \u2020 levy little or no general city rate. The five metro townships (Copperton, Emigration Canyon, Kearns, Magna, White City) fund municipal services through the Greater Salt Lake Municipal Services District, and Brighton contracts for its services the same way; Riverton and Herriman fund police and fire through their own separate service areas (adding 0.002198 and 0.002792 respectively, on top of the rate shown). Their bars therefore understate what residents actually pay for city services.",
      "The proposed-increase tab shows the ten Salt Lake County cities and towns that noticed a 2026 truth-in-taxation hike. As with the Utah County chart, Eagle Mountain is the only city with a rate that is not an estimate — it published a proposed rate and then adopted 0.001500 on August 6; the Salt Lake resulting rates are derived from each city's own noticed dollar impact, so treat them as estimates. School districts, water conservancy districts, and sub-city service areas that also noticed increases are left off to keep the comparison city-to-city."
    ],
